{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791195744,"nonce":"oBszV08pnEKx7SwLJWN9PKLiykQ=","title":"No CJK fallback face: Chinese, Japanese and Korean text draws the missing-glyph box","message":"Handed over by the ashui session (their f490b9d). Text like \"你好 こんにちは 안녕\"\ndraws the primary face's missing-glyph box for every character, because the\nfallback chain in blinc_text::fallback has only a symbol face and an emoji face.\nConfirmed earlier in this work: under a monospace primary a CJK character drew\nthe primary's notdef box, with no fallback attempted.\n\n\nA CJK fallback tried for characters the primary lacks, in the chain after the\nprimary and the existing faces. Candidates per platform, from ashui:\n\n- macOS: PingFang SC, PingFang TC, PingFang HK, Hiragino Sans, Apple SD\n  Gothic Neo\n- Linux: Noto Sans CJK\n- Windows: Microsoft YaHei, Yu Gothic, Malgun Gothic\n\nNo single face covers every script, so the choice is per character: the first\ncandidate that has the glyph. That also means a face is only loaded when a\ncharacter actually needs it, since CJK fonts are tens of megabytes.\n\n\n- Han unification: one codepoint, different glyph shapes for Simplified,\n  Traditional, Japanese and Korean. The first candidate wins, which is a\n  guess at the reader's locale. Choosing by locale or by an explicit language\n  is separate work.\n- Weight and italic are not carried to the fallback face, as with the symbol\n  and emoji faces.\n- Styled text (prepare_styled_text) draws on the primary face alone and does\n  not get this.\n\nBuilds on 124ad5d, which moved fallback decisions into layout.","files":null}]}